  <!-- ── Bio ───────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="about-bio">

    <p>Laurie Di Francesco is a photographer and writer. Her work moves between the documentary and the poetic, and always returns to the same question: what holds, when the ground gives way. Thresholds, displacements, bodies forced to reinvent their footing.</p>

    <p>Raised between France and Italy, she learned early the strength and fragility of thresholds, and came to question what it means to be rooted — a displacement that reaches all the way into the language she thinks in. The blue of the sky, the same from one end of the earth to the other, runs through her work as a possible anchor point.</p>

    <p>As a young adult, she moved to Brazil, a country of the heart and of expansion for her practice. Largely self-taught, she took her first photography courses at twenty in Rio de Janeiro, at Ateliê da Imagem. She later trained at ENSP in Arles, and refined her practice through workshops with Fabienne Pavia, Antoine d'Agata, and Klavdij Sluban, the latter in Slovenia. The rest, she learned by making images.</p>

    <p>Hers is a photography of the intimate. She works as close as possible to what is fragile, from within her subjects rather than at a distance from them. The conditions of making are part of the images: what the body could not choose, what chance decided, what the tools recorded on their own.</p>

    <p>A philosophy graduate, she reads Nietzsche, Ricœur and Bachelard, and works from a single conviction: the real can be transfigured without being idealized, in what it carries of light as much as fragility. Her images do not claim to transcribe the world. An image is always an interpretation, and she works this gap openly — with artefacts, imperfection, and a share of chance, never as an absence of mastery but as a way of asking what a photograph is really showing.</p>

    <p>Her first solo exhibition, <em>Spilling Beyond the Lines</em>, was held in Belo Horizonte in 2024. It brought together twenty years of photographs taken before she called herself a photographer, and looked for the thread running through them. Two bodies of work are ongoing: <em>Quando sono qui, non sono là</em>, on divided presence and what a childhood carries, and a cycle on the body and water, with <em>En Corps à la Mer</em> and <em>La Mer en Corps</em>.</p>

    <p>She is a member of Monolit, the collective founded by Oriane Ciantar Olive, where she works alongside other photographers to share resources, readings and ways of thinking, and to keep the practice moving.</p>

    <p class="bio-inspirations">Inspirations: Claudia Andujar, Graciela Iturbide, Luigi Ghirri,
    Jacques-Henri Lartigue, Manuel Álvarez Bravo, Miguel Rio Branco, José Diniz, Letizia
    Battaglia, among others.</p>

  </div>

</Layout>
